# DTF tier manifest: proxy-cache-empty-object  (artifact-keeper#4630, #4646, #4758, #4759)
# Profile-set: filesystem + a steerable static upstream (upstreams.mock-static:
# a stdlib-python server on a per-slot 172.16.<slot>.0/24 subnet that serves
# deterministic files, counts GETs per path, and can be told to promise N
# bytes and close early). Stored objects are damaged directly on the backend's
# /data volume through a throwaway postgres:16-alpine container with
# --volumes-from the backend (the backend image has no shell), as the
# storage-integrity tier does.
#
# THE BUG (#4630)
# A proxy-cache entry is two objects: __content__ and __cache_meta__.json.
# The streaming read path takes Content-Length from the sidecar's size_bytes
# and self-heals only when the body is MISSING (NotFound). In production a
# publish copy() landed a zero-byte __content__ while the sidecar recorded the
# real size and SHA-256 and pinned storage_etag = d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e
# (the MD5 of zero bytes). Every GET then answered 200 + Content-Length: N and
# aborted with no body, forever: the object exists, so the self-heal never
# fires. The fix (#4661) treats a sidecar that claims size_bytes > 0 while
# pinning the empty-object ETag as a miss, refetches, and rewrites the entry;
# on the write side it measures the published object after the copy.
#
# THE BUG (#4646)
# The filesystem backend's put/put_stream ended with sync_all() and no flush(),
# so a failed FINAL write was swallowed and a short object published. In the
# OCI chunked upload path that surfaced as a client 400 DIGEST_INVALID for
# bytes the server lost. The fix (#4749) adds the flush AND a per-part length
# check at commit: a stored part shorter than the size_bytes its PATCH
# recorded fails the commit as 500 BLOB_UPLOAD_UNKNOWN naming the part.
#
# THE BUGS (#4758, #4759; fixed by #4768)
# #4758: the #4661 heal keys on the empty-object ETag a sidecar pins, but the
# filesystem backend (the default) never pins one, so an emptied __content__
# under an intact sidecar was still served as 200 + Content-Length: N with no
# body and upstream was never contacted. The fix compares storage.size() with
# the sidecar's size_bytes when no ETag is pinned, evicts on a mismatch, and
# refetches.
# #4759: an upstream that advertised Content-Length: N and sent no body got
# its headers forwarded at once, so the client saw a dropped connection. The
# fix waits for the first body byte: none -> 502 {code: UPSTREAM_SHORT_BODY,
# message naming the repository and "promised N bytes ... after 0 bytes"},
# never the upstream URL. A body that ends after some bytes were forwarded
# still closes the connection (the headers are out), and the tee now logs a
# warn with the cache key, expected_bytes and received_bytes. Nothing is
# cached either way.
#
# THE PRIMITIVES
#   #4630 read  prime an entry through the Remote, then rewrite it on disk into
#               the exact #4630 state (empty __content__, sidecar size_bytes N,
#               storage_etag the empty-object MD5), wait out the backend's
#               30 s in-process sidecar LRU, GET.
#               fixed:  200 with the full upstream bytes, upstream contacted,
#                       __content__ rewritten to N bytes, then a cache hit.
#               1.11.1: 200 + Content-Length N, zero bytes (curl rc 18), no
#                       upstream request, entry unchanged.
#   #4758 read  prime an entry, empty its __content__ on disk, leave the
#               sidecar exactly as written (storage_etag absent), GET.
#               fixed:  200 with the full bytes, exactly one upstream GET,
#                       __content__ rewritten, then a cache hit.
#               1.11.1 / 6a4e29f1: 200 + Content-Length N, zero bytes
#                       (curl rc 18), no upstream request.
#   #4759       mock mode `empty` (Content-Length N, no body) and `short`
#               (half the body, close).
#               fixed:  empty -> 502 UPSTREAM_SHORT_BODY naming the repo and
#                       N/0; short -> curl rc 18 or 52 (52 when the close
#                       beats the header flush; scheduling, seen on 1.11.3
#                       and 1.11.4) and a docker-logs warn naming the path
#                       with expected_bytes=N, received_bytes<N.
#               1.11.1 / 6a4e29f1: empty -> no HTTP response (curl rc 52);
#                       short -> rc 18, no warn with the counts.
#   #4646       two 64 KiB PATCHes, truncate stored part 0 by 4 KiB, commit.
#               fixed:  500 BLOB_UPLOAD_UNKNOWN naming the part, no blob.
#               1.11.1: 400 DIGEST_INVALID ("computed ... != provided").
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      the primitives above. RED on 1.11.1 (all four) and on main
#              6a4e29f1 (#4758 and both #4759 cases).
#   (CONTROL)  must hold on BOTH images: a cold Remote fetch returns the
#              upstream bytes and caches, the next fetch is a cache hit (the
#              upstream counter does not move); an upstream that promises N
#              bytes and sends none / half is never committed as a cache entry
#              and caches normally once healthy (the pre-existing #1365/#1912
#              staging guards, which #4630's write-side measurement sits
#              behind); a clean two-PATCH OCI upload commits and reads back by
#              digest; a generic upload's stored object and X-Checksum-Sha256
#              match the uploaded bytes.
#
# The profile pins the backend's json-file log driver for the #4759 warn
# (rootless podman's journald driver drops lines) and runs the mock with
# label=disable (SELinux-enforcing hosts cannot read the bind-mounted script).
#
# NOT GATED HERE (see the PR): #4630's post-copy publish measurement needs a
# copy() that reports success and lands short, which the filesystem backend's
# honest copy cannot produce; #4646's flush() needs a final write that fails
# (ENOSPC/EIO/EFBIG) inside the backend container, which this compose topology
# cannot inject without a size-limited storage mount.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: ~40 authenticated calls back to back.
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the mock upstream is on a private subnet.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-static"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
